'''auspiciously''' (âs-pish′us-li), ''adv.'' In an auspicious manner; with favorable omens; happily; prosperously; favorably; propitiously.
{{smaller block|I looked for ruin; and encrease of honour
Meets me ''auspiciously.'' ''Middleton'', Witch, iv. 1.}}

'''auspiciousness''' (âs-pish′us-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being auspicious; a state of fair promise; prosperity.

'''auster''' (âs′tėr), ''n.'' [L., the south wind; akin to ''aurora'', the dawn, and ''urere'', burn: see ''aurora'' and ''aurum''.] '''1.''' The south wind (commonly with a capital, as a proper name): as, “drizzly ''Auster''.” ''Thomson'', Castle of Indolence, lxxvi. Hence—'''2†.''' The south.

