'''austerely''' (âs-tēr′li), ''adv.'' In an austere manner; severely; rigidly; harshly.
{{smaller block|<poem>Whatever hypocrites ''austerely'' talk
Of purity. {{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 744.}}</poem>
In the wonder whether a door so grimly bolted and ''austerely'' barred could possibly open into a hotel, with cheerful overcharges for candles and service.
{{right|''Howells'', Venetian Life, ii.}}}}

'''austereness''' (âs-tēr′nes), ''n.'' [ME. ''austernesse'' (Wyclif); < ''austere'' + ''-ness.''] The state or quality of being austere; harshness in taste; severity in manners; austerity.
{{smaller block|<poem>For a subject
Towards his prince, in things indifferent
To use th’ ''austereness'' of a censuring Cato
Is arrogance, not freedom.
{{right|''Fletcher (and others)'', Bloody Brother, v. 1.}}</poem>}}

'''austerity''' (âs-ter′i-ti), ''n.''; pl. ''austerities'' (-tiz). [< ME. ''austerite'', < OF. ''austerite'', F. ''austérité'', < ML. ''austerita(t-)s'', < L. ''austerus'', austere: see ''austere.''] '''1†.''' Harshness or astringency of taste.
{{smaller block|The sweetness of the ripened fruit is not the less delicious for the ''austerity'' of its crude state.
{{right|''Horsley'', Sermons, II. xxviii.}}}}
'''2.''' Severity of manner, life, etc.; rigor; strictness; harshness of treatment or demeanor.
{{smaller block|But the ''austerity'' of Dante will not condescend to the conventional elegance which makes the charm of French.}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 2d ser., p. 23.}}
There is no show of mercy in him. He carried his ''austerity'' beyond the bounds of humanity.
{{right|''Whipple'', Ess. and Rev., I. 20.}}}}
'''3.''' Severe or rigorous simplicity; absence of adornment or luxury.
{{smaller block|The Baptist we know was a strict man, remarkable for ''austerity'' and set order of life.
{{right|''Milton'', Apology for Smectymnuus.}}}}
'''4.''' Severe or ascetic practices: chiefly in the plural: as, the ''austerities'' of the Flagellants.
{{smaller block|The ''austerities'' and the blameless purity of Ximenes’s life had given him a reputation for sanctity throughout Spain.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 5.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Self-sacrifice'', ''Asceticism'', etc. (see ''self-denial''); sternness, harshness. See comparison under ''austere''.}}

'''austerland''' (âs′tėr-land), ''n.'' [E. dial., < *''auster'' = ''astre'', hearth (see ''astre''), + ''land''.] Land which had a house upon it in ancient times. ''Elton'', Origins of Eng. Hist., p. 191. [Local Eng.]

'''Austin''' (âs′tin), ''a.'' [< ME. ''Austyn'', contr. of ''Augustin'', q. v.] Same as ''Augustinian'': as, ''Austin'' friars. See ''Augustin''.

'''austral''' (âs′tral), ''a.'' [< ME. ''austral'' = F. Sp. Pg. ''austral'' = It. ''australe'', < L. ''australis'', southern, < ''auster'', the south wind: see ''auster''.] Southern; lying in or pertaining to the south: as, ''austral'' lands; the ''austral'' signs of the zodiac.—{{smaller|'''Austral pole''', the name given by French authors to that pole of a magnet which points to the north, and is called the north pole by English and American writers. So, also, what is termed the south pole by the latter is termed the ''boreal'' pole by the former.—'''Austral signs''', the last six signs of the zodiac, or those south of the equator.}}

